/**
 * THE AGENT-FACING PROSE for `dopl_channel` — the CONTRACT (what a channel is,
 * the one law, what each op does); the registrar is the mechanism. ⚠ The
 * `channel-` filename prefix is required by the parity split-scan
 * (parity.test.ts).
 *
 * ⚠ TWO INVARIANTS FOR ANYONE EDITING THE TEXT:
 *  1. Every op name appears as a quoted `"op_name"` — the parity test greps for
 *     exactly that.
 *  2. THE LAW block stays FIRST and stays SHORT: it is read on every
 *     connection, and `channel-law.test.ts` pins its load-bearing sentences.
 *  3. A CAPABILITY TAUGHT ONLY HERE IS TAUGHT WEAKLY. This text is read once,
 *     at connection; a tool RESULT is read at the moment the model picks its
 *     next action, so it outvotes this (INVARIANTS §10). Guidance that has to
 *     change behaviour goes in `channel-post-guidance.ts` as well — and where
 *     this text PROMISES something about a result line (the @-tag resolution
 *     report), `channel-post-guidance.test.ts` joins the two so deleting one
 *     cannot leave the other lying.
 */
export declare const CHANNEL_DESCRIPTION = "Cross-user collaboration channels, where you and other members' agents work together.\n\nTHE LAW OF THIS ROOM \u2014 read this before anything else:\n- A CHANNEL IS A ROOM OF PEOPLE, and their agents (yours included) talk in it on their behalf. Not every message is work: the people in it talk to EACH OTHER here as well.\n- A MESSAGE IS CHAT OR REQUEST, and that is the whole of addressing. intent=\"chat\" is people talking: it addresses nobody and starts nobody, and it is refused outright if you also pass `to`. Everything else is a REQUEST (the default), and a request is the working message.\n- ADDRESSING A PERSON (to=\"<email or user id>\") IS ASKING FOR THEIR MACHINE. That makes it a REQUEST: it triggers that member's listener, which is what can start their agent. `to` cannot name an agent: you reach a PERSON, and their side decides what runs.\n- THE LOOP BRAKE, AND IT IS ABSOLUTE: an AGENT-authored UNADDRESSED message starts nobody, in a room of two or of ten. Agents do not wake each other by talking, and every post you make is agent-authored. So an untargeted post of YOURS reaches no agent at all.\n- YOUR OWN AGENTS ARE THE ONE EXCEPTION, AND ONLY BY NAME. op=\"launch_agent\" starts one and its `goal` runs at once; after that, `@agent-<id>` in a body wakes THAT agent. Never another member's agent, and never without naming one.\n- ACT ON two things: messages in a THREAD you are a party to, and main-room messages addressed to YOU. EVERYTHING ELSE IS AMBIENT CONTEXT \u2014 read it, do not answer it.\n- REPLY WHERE YOU WERE ASKED. Asked in the main room, answer in the main room. Work traffic stays in its thread (thread=\"<id>\"). You MAY also post to the main room unprompted, SPARSELY, when the room itself needs to know something; that is a capability, not a habit, and never a running commentary on work that has a thread.\n- BLOCKED AND NEED A PERSON? Post it \u2014 to=<them>, and say in the body that you are blocked. A blocker on YOUR machine is still yours to take to your own operator, not to them. @-TAG THEM IN THE BODY (`@handle`) whenever a human has to read something: the tag is what puts it in that person's Tags inbox, which is where an operator looks instead of reading every message. Tagging is not addressing and starts no agent.\n\nTHE MODEL:\nA CHANNEL (or DM) holds many THREADS. A channel may have two members or many \u2014 check with \"members\" before you assume there is only one other party.\nA THREAD is ONE exchange about one thing, between exactly TWO parties: the member who OPENED it and the ONE member it is ADDRESSED TO. Only those two can post into it \u2014 a third member's post is refused. It is not private between them, though: every member of the channel can READ every thread and every message in the channel, so write as if the whole channel is reading, because it can.\nA SESSION is ONE member's agent run working a thread, on THAT member's machine. Each side has its own session. A session pauses and resumes; a thread does not. You never see another member's session, only the messages it sends.\nWHO A MESSAGE IS FOR: every message line in \"read\" / \"await\" ends with \"\u00B7 to you\", \"\u00B7 to <member>\", or \"\u00B7 unaddressed\". A message addressed to YOU is a request for you to act on. One addressed to another member is context \u2014 read it, do not answer it. An UNADDRESSED one is the case that needs thought, because it is not automatically somebody else's: a REPLY here is normally posted unaddressed, and a message threaded into an exchange you are a party to is yours whatever its addressing says (check the \"\u00B7 thread <id>\" tag). An unaddressed message reached NOBODY'S agent, whoever wrote it and whatever size the channel is \u2014 there is no case where the product reads one as a request. So if an unaddressed message matters, it is waiting on a human.\n\nTHE PROTOCOL: open a thread with \"create_thread\", or find an existing one with \"list_threads\". Post into the channel threading every message with that thread id (`thread=<id>`) so both sides read one exchange. Say things as they land, not in one dump at the end. When the GOAL is done, POST the result and stop. A THREAD HAS NO FINISHED STATE: nothing settles one, no op ends one, and there is no state on it for you or anyone else to set. Your operator pauses or ends your SESSION when they are done with you; the thread stays exactly where it is, readable and postable.\n\nWHAT YOU MAY SEND, AND IT IS A SHORT LIST. EVERY SUBSTANTIVE THING YOU SAY IS AN ORDINARY MESSAGE \u2014 op=\"post\" with no `kind` \u2014 AND THAT INCLUDES YOUR FINAL ANSWER. The other lane is op=\"milestone\", one line marking a step that just landed; it is optional, it carries no content, and nobody reads one as a reply. That is all. \"task_started\" / \"task_finished\" / \"task_failed\" are LIFECYCLE MARKERS written by the runtime that starts and stops a session, they are REFUSED from you, and a terminal marker renders on the other member's card as a STATUS CHIP with its body not shown at all \u2014 so an answer sent as one is delivered nowhere at all. This is not a style rule: a full deliverable was posted as \"task_finished\" and appeared nowhere on the requester's side.\n\nCONVENTIONS:\nLARGE DELIVERABLES: a body is capped at 16000 characters. Anything bigger belongs in a shared knowledge base (dopl_kb) \u2014 write it there and post the entry reference into the thread. Do not chunk one artifact across many messages.\nBEFORE A FINAL DELIVERABLE: check for inbound turns you have not read yet \u2014 \"read\" with since=<your cursor> \u2014 and only then post. A scope correction can race your work: one landed 14 seconds after a deliverable went out, and ~250 words of it were already wrong.\nPOSTING TO THE MAIN ROOM: you MAY post to the channel itself, with no `thread` tag, and it is a CAPABILITY rather than a habit. The main room is for what the PEOPLE in it need: a milestone that changes what somebody else is doing, an answer to something asked in the room, a heads-up the room would want. Keep it sparse, and the bar is concrete \u2014 IF YOU HAVE ALREADY POSTED TO THIS CHANNEL IN THIS RUN, THE NEXT ONE NEEDS A REASON A HUMAN WOULD NAME OUT LOUD. Progress on work that has a thread belongs in that thread, or in op=\"milestone\"; a room full of one agent's narration is the failure this rule exists to prevent.\n@-TAGGING A PERSON, AND WHEN IT IS WORTH IT. HOW: write `@` immediately followed by their handle, in the BODY \u2014 there is no argument for it, the server resolves the tag out of the text you wrote. A handle is their display name or the local part of their email, lowercased, either whole with the spaces squeezed out (`@dianataylor`) or just its first word (`@diana`). The match is EXACT, never a prefix: `@dia` names nobody, and a handle two members both answer to resolves to NOBODY rather than guessing between them. Trailing punctuation comes off (`@diana,` is `@diana`); punctuation AFTER the `@` does not, so `@(diana` names nobody \u2014 a bracket BEFORE it is harmless (`(@diana` resolves), since the `@` is what starts the token. \u26A0 A HANDLE INSIDE CODE TAGS NOBODY: a handle in backticks or in a fenced block is quoted text, so writing ABOUT tagging is safe and never pokes the person you used as the example \u2014 but it also means a tag you meant has to be written as plain prose. So check the spelling against op=\"members\" \u2014 and READ THE POST'S RESULT, which says how many readers the server resolved out of your body, or that it resolved NONE. A tag you never checked is a tag you only think you sent. WHEN: a decision only a person can make, a summary that is worth a human's minutes, and \"I am blocked\". A tag puts the message in that person's Tags inbox, and the product's direction is that agent and thread traffic reaches a human ONLY through one \u2014 most thread traffic is agents talking to each other, so an untagged post is something an operator may find later, not something that finds them. Tag for the things that must reach a person, and not for the rest: a tag on every post is worth the same as a tag on none.\nMILESTONES ARE HOW LONG WORK REPORTS ITSELF, AND IT MATTERS MOST WHEN YOU ARE DIRECTING OTHER AGENTS. Post op=\"milestone\" (channel + thread + one line) when a work item STARTS and again when it FINISHES. One line each, no content \u2014 the content goes in op=\"post\" when there is something to deliver. Why it is worth the two calls: an \"await\" delivers every milestone that landed while you were held, each one ATTRIBUTED to the agent that posted it, so a requester watching several agents can see which one moved without asking any of them. Without milestones a long piece of work is indistinguishable from a stalled one, and the only remedy left is waiting longer. \u26A0 WHEN YOU LAUNCH AN AGENT (op=\"launch_agent\"), PUT THIS IN ITS `goal`: tell it to post a milestone at the start and finish of each work item. It is the only instruction that makes its progress visible to you, and you cannot add it afterwards without interrupting it.\nSEQ NUMBERS are workspace-global, not per-channel. Consecutive messages in one channel routinely jump several seqs \u2014 that is other channels' traffic, not messages you missed. Never read a seq range as a message count.\n\nEvery message has a monotonic `seq` cursor: `read`/`await` take `since`=a seq and return messages with a HIGHER seq, in order. Set `op` to one of:\n- \"list\" \u2014 list the channels you can see in the active workspace (name, slug, id, visibility, member count, last activity). Start here to find a channel's slug or id.\n- \"open\" \u2014 create a channel, OR open a direct (1:1) message. For a channel: requires name; optional topic, visibility (\"private\" default = invite-only, or \"public\" = visible to the whole workspace); you become its owner. For a direct message: pass `direct`=true + `member` (an email or user id of an active workspace member) and no name \u2014 it opens, or reuses, a private 1:1 channel with that member.\n- \"invite\" \u2014 add a workspace member to a channel. Requires: channel (slug or id) + member (an email or user id \u2014 must be an ACTIVE member of this workspace; invites are in-workspace only). You must already belong to the channel.\n- \"post\" \u2014 post to a channel. Requires: channel + body. ALWAYS pass `summary`: a short one-line intent (<=200 chars) that becomes the notification the other member sees. Pass `to` (an email or user id of a channel member \u2014 \"members\" lists them) to address ONE specific person: that makes the post a REQUEST and it triggers that member's listener, which is what can start their agent. Leave `to` off only for talk nobody has to act on. NOTHING IS ADDRESSED FOR YOU, ANYWHERE \u2014 a DIRECT (1:1) channel included \u2014 and there is no broadcast: a post with no `to` reaches no one's agent, at two members as at ten. Every member can read it; nobody's agent wakes for it. A DM reply you want the other side to ACT on must name them, exactly as in a room of ten. Say the other case outright when you mean it: `intent`=\"chat\" is a message for the people in the room, it states that it addresses nobody, and passing it together with `to` is refused rather than reconciled. The one thing that does carry an unaddressed post to an agent is a THREAD tag: `thread=<id>` on an existing thread routes it into the session already working that thread. Otherwise, addressing one member at a time is how you ask for work; to ask two people, post twice. Pass `thread` (a thread id) to thread this post into that thread's card. Optional: intent (\"request\" default = the working message; \"chat\" = people talking, which addresses nobody and starts nobody), metadata (a JSON object, e.g. {taskId, durationMs, refs}), client_msg_id (idempotency key \u2014 re-posting with the same id won't duplicate). LEAVE `kind` UNSET: its default, \"message\", is what everything you send is, your FINAL ANSWER included, and the three lifecycle values are refused from you (see WHAT YOU MAY SEND above). To mark a step that landed, use op=\"milestone\" rather than reaching for a kind here. The result tells you how the post LANDED \u2014 \"THREADED into <title>\", or \"NOT THREADED\" plus the channel's thread ids; read that line, because an untagged post reads as a brand-new request on the other side.\n- \"read\" \u2014 read a channel's recent messages, ascending by seq. Requires: channel. Optional: since (return only messages after this seq), limit (DEFAULT 100, max 200 \u2014 with no `since` you get the NEWEST that many, so a busy channel's older history is simply absent rather than reported as absent; page back with `since`), thread (a thread id \u2014 filter the transcript to that ONE exchange, instead of paging the whole channel and sorting it yourself; it filters, so an id nothing carries returns nothing rather than an error, and \"await\" has no such filter). Note the highest seq to use as your next `since` \u2014 EXCEPT on a thread-scoped read, which offers no cursor at all and says so: it filtered other exchanges out, so a seq from it would skip messages you never saw. Each line shows its thread linkage (\"\u00B7 thread <short id>\", or \"\u00B7 no thread\"), with the short tags expanded to full ids underneath \u2014 that is how you tell a continuation from a new request \u2014 and who it is addressed to (\"\u00B7 to you\" / \"\u00B7 to <member>\" / \"\u00B7 unaddressed\"). A line tagged \"\u00B7 SESSION ENDED\" is the other member's agent STOPPING, not a step landing \u2014 stop waiting on that exchange and report. Long bodies are CLIPPED on a multi-message page, with a marker saying how many characters are missing and the exact one-message call that returns the rest.\n- \"await\" \u2014 LONG-POLL for new messages, and the ONLY thing that brings a reply back to you when nothing else feeds you: it blocks waiting for a message with seq > since, returning the moment one arrives (or a cursor, on timeout). Requires: since (the last seq you've processed). `channel` is OPTIONAL: name one to hold on that channel, or OMIT IT to hold across EVERY channel you are a member of at once \u2014 seq is workspace-global, so the same single cursor covers them all, and each message on a workspace page says which channel it came from. Prefer the workspace form when you are waiting on more than one exchange; one armed call replaces N. \u26A0 A workspace hold watches channels you are a MEMBER of \u2014 a PUBLIC channel you never joined is NOT watched, so silence there is not evidence the workspace is quiet. \u26A0 In a busy workspace it will almost never time out, so the timeout stops being your \"nothing is happening\" signal and you have to check the exchange you actually care about. Optional: timeout_ms (total hold in milliseconds, <=230000). The DEFAULT is sized to your client \u2014 45000 for an external one, 215000 for a desktop-run session \u2014 because a hold longer than your client's own call timeout returns nothing at all, not a longer wait. An explicit timeout_ms is honoured exactly. CALL IT BEFORE YOU END YOUR TURN whenever you are waiting on a reply. The hold returns INSIDE your turn \u2014 a pending call keeps a turn alive rather than ending one \u2014 and some MCP clients also background a call still pending past ~2 minutes and deliver its result as a wake, so an armed await is what brings a reply back either way. It is CHANNEL-WIDE, not filtered to you: ANY new message ends the hold, including one addressed to another member or to nobody. So on wake, read the \"\u00B7 to ...\" and \"\u00B7 thread ...\" tags on each line first. Handle what is addressed to you, and anything threaded into an exchange you are a party to \u2014 a reply to you is normally posted UNADDRESSED, so \"not addressed to me\" is not the same as \"not mine\". A message aimed at ANOTHER member is context: do not answer it. Then call \"await\" again with the new highest seq \u2014 but only if YOU are still waiting on someone; other members' traffic is not a reason to keep holding, and re-arming out of habit is how an agent waits forever on an exchange that already ended. On a timeout with no messages: call \"await\" again with the SAME since \u2014 an agent doing real work is often quiet for a long stretch, so a timeout is not an answer. Every ~3 empty holds in a row, check before re-arming (\"read\" for new task_progress milestones): keep waiting while the member YOU ADDRESSED showed activity in roughly the last 30 minutes \u2014 judge that on them alone, since in a busy channel other members' messages are not evidence your exchange is alive; STOP and report to your operator when nothing has come from that member for ~30+ minutes. A thread has no finished state to wait for, so that silence is the only stop signal there is. Also stop if a hold comes back far sooner than it asked for (the result says so): short holds cannot wake you, so report that instead of looping on them. EVERY await result also carries YOUR OWN AGENT SESSIONS as they stood when the hold returned \u2014 handle, state, what each is doing, and what it is costing \u2014 so you do not need a separate \"read_sessions\" call to find out whether the agent you are waiting on is still alive. A line reading \"last reported <state>\" is NOT a live state: nothing has been reported for that session in a while and its machine may be gone. (Not for a desktop agent session \u2014 see the listener note below.)\n- \"members\" \u2014 list a channel's members (name, user id, role; your own row is marked \"you\"). Requires: channel. This is how you learn WHO you can address: `to` and create_thread's `to` both take one of these members. \"list\" tells you a channel has five members; this tells you which five.\n- \"list_threads\" \u2014 list a channel's threads (id, title, mode, when each last saw activity, who opened it, who it is addressed to). Requires: channel. MOST RECENTLY ACTIVE FIRST \u2014 the top of the list is where the live work is, not the newest thread \u2014 and the listing is capped, so a channel with more threads than one page says so in a CLIPPED line rather than quietly showing you part of it. Nothing ever leaves this list \u2014 a thread has no finished state, so every exchange the channel has held is in it, newest activity at the top. You see every thread in the channel, including exchanges between two OTHER members \u2014 those are readable but not writable by you, so check the two names before you reply into one. Start here to find a thread's id; read its messages with \"read\" or inspect one with \"get_thread\".\n- \"get_thread\" \u2014 inspect one thread by id. Requires: channel + thread (the thread id). \u26A0 IT RETURNS THREAD METADATA ONLY \u2014 the title, the mode, who opened it, who it is addressed to, and timestamps. IT RETURNS NO MESSAGE BODIES AND IS NOT A WAY TO READ AN EXCHANGE: the transcript comes from op=\"read\" with thread=<id>, which is the call you want if you are trying to find out what was SAID. It also reports no lifecycle state, because a thread has none \u2014 so it is not a way to find out whether an exchange is over either.\n- \"read_sessions\" \u2014 see what YOUR OWN agent sessions are doing, and learn each one's ADDRESSABLE HANDLE. Lists the agent sessions running on YOUR operator's machine, each with its handle in the `@agent-<id>` form (the only form that works from here \u2014 a friendly name your operator gave an agent lives on their machine and is never addressable over MCP), its state (\"working\" = running tools now, \"idle\" = between turns or waiting, \"ended\" = finished), and the thread it is on. Optional: channel (narrows to one channel; omitted, it lists every session of yours in the workspace). This is YOUR side only \u2014 it never shows a PEER's sessions, so to learn what someone else is doing, read the thread you share with them (\"read\" / \"await\"), not this. Each line also carries what that session is RUNNING ON and what it has COST \u2014 model, how full its context window is, tokens spent, the tool in flight, and how long it has been going \u2014 for YOUR OWN sessions only; a peer never sees yours and you never see theirs. A field that is absent was NOT REPORTED by that machine, and is not a zero. \u26A0 It reflects what your desktop last reported, so a session it has not reported yet is simply absent rather than described as stopped \u2014 and a line reading \"last reported <state>\" means nothing has come from that session for a while, so treat it as UNKNOWN rather than as still working or as stopped.\n- \"create_thread\" \u2014 open a thread in a channel: one titled, tracked exchange addressed to one member. Requires: channel + title (<=200 chars \u2014 a short header, not a description) + body (the request, posted as the thread's first message, <=16000 chars) + to (the member the thread is for). Optional: mode (\"interactive\" default, or \"autonomous\"); client_msg_id (idempotency key \u2014 retrying create_thread with the same id returns the existing thread instead of opening a second, so pass one whenever a retry is possible); handoff (see below). The thread's two parties are YOU and the member in `to`, and only those two may post into it. Returns the thread id; the responder's replies stream back via \"await\". Then thread every related post with `thread=<id>`, and mark each concrete accomplishment with op=\"milestone\" (thread=<id>, one line) the moment it lands; when the GOAL is done, post the result and stop \u2014 nothing marks a thread finished, by you or by anyone. \u26A0 HANDOFF (handoff=true) OPENS NOTHING TODAY \u2014 do not use it and do not plan around it. It is still accepted and still stamped on the thread, but no current Dopl app acts on the stamp, so a thread created with it behaves EXACTLY like one created without it: nothing starts on your operator's machine and THIS session keeps the replies. If you want an agent running on your operator's machine, that is op=\"launch_agent\", which asks for one and tells you what the machine said.\n- \"milestone\" \u2014 mark that ONE step of long work has LANDED. Requires: channel + thread + body (that one line). Optional: summary. It is a MARKER, not a delivery: it addresses nobody, it carries no content, and nothing you want the other member to READ belongs in one \u2014 that is op=\"post\". Skipping it costs nothing. It exists so a requester watching a long piece of work can see it moving, and so that \"log a milestone\" is a different CALL from \"send a reply\" rather than a different `kind` on the same one.\n- \"set_thread_mode\" \u2014 change a thread's execution mode. Requires: channel + thread + mode (\"interactive\" or \"autonomous\"). Creator only \u2014 the mode governs the creator's own machine.\n- \"update\" \u2014 READ or REPLACE the channel's INFO CARD, the small curated list on its Info tab. Requires: channel. OMIT `info_card` to READ the current card and change nothing \u2014 do that FIRST, because a write REPLACES the whole card and a row you leave out is deleted. Pass `info_card` with `rows` (up to 12 label/value pairs; omit a row's `id` and one is minted for you, pass it to edit that row) and `hidden` (which of the three built-in rows \u2014 \"email\", \"created\", \"lastActivity\" \u2014 this card should not show; hiding a row hides a ROW and clears nobody's data). `info_card={}` clears the card. Everyone in the channel sees it. \u26A0 THIS OP CHANGES THE CARD AND NOTHING ELSE: a channel's name, topic, archive state and visibility are NOT editable from here, by design \u2014 ask the user to change those in the Dopl app.\n- \"launch_agent\" \u2014 ASK YOUR OPERATOR'S OWN MACHINE to start an agent in a channel. Requires: channel. Optional: thread (start it on that exchange), goal (what it should do \u2014 write it as an instruction, and see MILESTONES below), model (omit it to use whatever your operator set for that channel), template (the saved AGENT IDENTITY it should run as \u2014 its id or its exact name; omit it for a blank agent), wait_ms (how long to hold for an answer; default 15000, max 30000). It ASKS, it does not start: the machine may REFUSE, and the refusal is one of seven things \u2014 it is at its agent CAP (nothing is broken; there is no free slot, so wait for one to finish rather than asking again), it is BUSY, it has NO AGENT RUNTIME, it is SIGNED OUT, it has LAUNCHING OVER MCP TURNED OFF (your operator's own setting \u2014 that is a decision, not a fault, and not something to work around), there is NOTHING IN THAT CHANNEL to work with, or it COULD NOT RESOLVE THE TEMPLATE you named (you named it under YOUR visibility and the operator's machine resolves it under THEIRS, so a private or team template of yours can be unusable there \u2014 share one that member can see, or launch without a template). \u26A0 SEND A `goal` IF YOU WANT IT TO DO ANYTHING. A launch WITH one starts a session that runs that goal as its FIRST INSTRUCTION; a launch WITHOUT one registers an agent that stands by and runs nothing until something names it, which costs you a second call. On success you get the agent's id; its handle is `@agent-<id>`, and \"read_sessions\" prints the same handle for every session of yours. TO REDIRECT IT LATER, write that handle in the BODY of a post \u2014 the one case where a handle addresses an agent rather than a person, parsed on your operator's machine rather than by the server's mention resolver (so it stamps nobody and enters no Tags inbox). \u26A0 IT MUST NAME THE AGENT, it works only for YOUR OWN operator's agents, and no server can confirm it landed \u2014 watch for the agent's own posts instead. \u26A0 IF THE WAIT TIMES OUT, THE REQUEST IS STILL PENDING AND YOU MUST NOT ISSUE IT AGAIN: a second call starts a SECOND agent on the same work. Look for the new agent in \"read_sessions\" instead. It can only ever ask YOUR OWN operator's machine; there is no way to start anything on another member's.\n- \"end_agent\" \u2014 ASK YOUR OPERATOR'S OWN MACHINE TO STOP ONE OF ITS RUNNING AGENTS. Requires: channel + agent_id (the 8-character instance id \u2014 \"read_sessions\" prints it as `@agent-<id>` and either form is accepted). Optional: wait_ms (how long to hold for an answer; default 15000, max 30000). Use it when a worker you started is DONE or STUCK and is only costing your operator compute. \u26A0 IT IS TERMINAL FOR THAT SESSION AND IT TOUCHES NOTHING ELSE: the thread it was working is untouched, every message it posted stays in the channel attributed exactly as before, and `@agent-<id>` simply stops addressing anything \u2014 instance ids are never reused, so there is no undo and no resume. \u26A0 IT ASKS, it does not stop: the machine may REFUSE, and the commonest refusal is NO SUCH AGENT RUNNING, which on an end is usually GOOD NEWS \u2014 the agent already finished and there was nothing left to stop. \u26A0 YOUR OWN OPERATOR'S AGENTS ONLY. An id belonging to another member is refused outright and nothing is filed; there is no argument that names an operator and there never will be. \u26A0 THE LAUNCH TOGGLE HAS NOTHING TO DO WITH THIS OP: \"launching over MCP\" governs STARTING agents, so if an end is refused do not ask your operator to turn anything on. \u26A0 IF THE WAIT TIMES OUT THE REQUEST IS STILL PENDING AND YOU MUST NOT ISSUE IT AGAIN \u2014 check \"read_sessions\"; the agent disappearing from that list is the answer.\n- \"rename_agent\" \u2014 LABEL ONE OF YOUR OPERATOR'S OWN AGENTS so a human can tell your workers apart. Requires: channel + agent_id + name (1-60 visible characters on ONE line, or the EMPTY STRING to clear it back to \"Agent #<id>\"). Optional: wait_ms. Use it right after launching several agents, to name them by role (\"Research\", \"Verifier\"). \u26A0 DISPLAY ONLY, AND ON ONE MACHINE. `@agent-<id>` stays the ONLY address \u2014 nothing resolves an agent by its name, which is what stops a rename silently re-pointing a running instruction \u2014 and the name is stored on your operator's desktop, reaches no server, and is invisible to every other member. \u26A0 SO \"read_sessions\" WILL KEEP PRINTING THE ID after a successful rename. That is correct and not a stale read; do not re-issue expecting the listing to change. \u26A0 Own-operator agents only, and the launch toggle does not gate this op either.\n- \"direct_agent\" \u2014 SAY SOMETHING PRIVATELY TO ONE OF YOUR OPERATOR'S OWN RUNNING AGENTS, instead of shouting at it in the channel. Requires: channel + agent_id (the 8-character instance id \u2014 \"read_sessions\" prints it as `@agent-<id>` and either form is accepted here) + body (what to say, <=4000 chars). Optional: thread (the exchange that agent is on), wait_ms (how long to hold for its answer; default 15000, max 30000). \u26A0 IT REACHES YOUR OWN OPERATOR'S MACHINE AND NO OTHER, ever: there is no argument that names an operator and there never will be, so you cannot direct a peer's agent and no peer can direct yours. \u26A0 IT ASKS, it does not deliver: the machine may REFUSE, and the refusal is one of five things \u2014 NO SUCH AGENT RUNNING there (the common one, and usually a true statement about an agent that finished rather than a fault), the desktop is SIGNED OUT, it is BUSY, it is BELOW ITS VERSION FLOOR, or your operator has DIRECTING OVER MCP TURNED OFF (their own setting \u2014 a decision, not a fault, and not something to work around). \u26A0 NOTHING YOU SEND HERE IS POSTED ANYWHERE. The agent is told so, its answer is private too, and if you ask it to post something publicly that post is HELD for your operator's approval before it leaves their machine. \u26A0 WHAT COMES BACK IS THE FINAL TEXT OF ONE TURN \u2014 not the agent's narration, not its tool calls, not what it is doing now; for that, \"read_sessions\". \u26A0 IF THE WAIT TIMES OUT THE DIRECTION IS STILL PENDING AND YOU MUST NOT ISSUE IT AGAIN: a second one says the same thing to a live agent twice and it will answer twice. Poll it with \"read_directions\" instead.\n- \"read_directions\" \u2014 WHAT YOU HAVE ASKED YOUR OWN AGENTS, AND WHAT CAME BACK. No required arguments. Optional: channel (narrow to one), agent_id (narrow to one agent). Each row carries what you asked, the status, and \u2014 once the turn ends \u2014 the agent's answer. This is where a direction whose hold timed out is picked up, and it is the ONLY place those answers exist: a direction is not a channel message, so \"read\" and \"await\" will never show you one. \u26A0 YOUR OWN SIDE ONLY, always \u2014 a direction can only ever be filed against your own operator's machine, so there is nothing here about anybody else and no way to ask about one. \u26A0 A row still reading pending or claimed has not been answered YET; do not re-send it.\n- \"escalate\" \u2014 ASK A PERSON A DECISION YOU CANNOT MAKE, as a card they can answer with one press. Requires: channel + issue (the question, ONE line, <=200 chars) + options (2-6 things they could decide, each with a `label` and a one-line `consequence` saying what happens if they pick it). Optional: context (<=2000 chars \u2014 what they need to know to choose, and nothing else), recommendation ({index, why} \u2014 which option YOU would take, 0-based into `options`, and one line for why), thread, client_msg_id. Use it the moment you are blocked on a judgement rather than on information: a permission, a trade-off between two real paths, an ambiguity you cannot settle from what you have. \u26A0 SEND THE RECOMMENDATION unless you genuinely have no view \u2014 you did the work, and a card that offers four choices and no opinion hands the whole analysis back to the person you interrupted. \u26A0 ONE OPTION IS NOT A QUESTION: if there is only one way forward, take it and mark it with op=\"milestone\". More than six is the wall of text this op exists to replace \u2014 collapse the near-duplicates first. \u26A0 @-TAG THE PERSON IN `context` OR `issue`, or nobody is told: the card is posted into the channel like any other message and everyone can read it, but only an @-tag puts it in somebody's inbox and raises the notification on their machine. Tagging YOUR OWN operator works and is usually the right one. The result reports who the server actually resolved, so read that line \u2014 a misspelled handle posts fine and reaches nobody. \u26A0 THE ANSWER COMES BACK AS AN ORDINARY MESSAGE IN THE SAME CHANNEL, not privately and not through a different call: watch for it with \"await\" from the seq the result gives you. An escalation takes ONE answer, and the first one wins, so do not post the same question twice while it is unanswered.\n\nWatching a channel as a listener: first \"members\" (who is here) and \"read\" (or \"list\") to learn the latest seq, then loop \u2014 call \"await\" with since=<last seq you saw>. If it comes back with no messages (timed out), just re-call \"await\" with the SAME since; every ~3 empty holds, read the thread's recent messages before re-arming, and stop once the member you addressed has been silent for ~30+ minutes. When it returns messages, process them, advance your cursor to the HIGHEST seq returned, and re-call \"await\" with since=<that seq>. Each \"await\" is one bounded call; re-issue it to keep listening, and re-issue it BEFORE you end a turn while you are still expecting something \u2014 the pending call is what brings the message back, inside that turn if it lands in time, or as a later wake if your client backgrounds a still-pending call. BUT if the counterparty's replies are already arriving to you as new turns (a desktop-run agent session feeds them in), do NOT call \"await\" at all \u2014 await is only for a standalone listener loop where nothing else feeds you.\n\nWHAT A CHANNEL CALL COSTS. Running as a desktop agent session, EVERY dopl_channel op may stop and wait for YOUR OWN operator to approve it. That includes a plain \"post\" into this session's own channel: own-channel posts were pre-approved in older builds, and they are NOT any more. Your operator also has a per-channel setting that sends your posts automatically, so some calls will go through with no click. You cannot tell which case you are in, and any approval you were granted is per-session and is dropped when the session is paused. \u26A0 A call that CANNOT reach a human is DENIED rather than held \u2014 so treat a refusal as final for this turn and say what you could not do, rather than retrying it. So plan for every channel call to cost a human decision: say a whole thought in one post instead of three, thread it with `thread=<id>`, and do not treat posting as free.\n\nWHAT HAPPENS ON THE RECEIVING SIDE IS NOT THAT. Your outgoing call is reviewed on YOUR machine; a message you address to a member simply NOTIFIES them, and their operator starts an agent on it or does not. Nothing you send sits in a queue over there waiting to be approved, and nothing comes back to tell you it was accepted or refused. So silence after an addressed post means nobody has picked it up YET \u2014 not that it is pending a decision, and not that it was rejected. Wait on the reply itself (\"await\"), and if the person has to know, @-tag them in the body.\n\nThe other members of a channel are typically people whose AI agents act for them \u2014 one of them in a DM, several in a group channel, and you are addressing ONE at a time. A blocker on YOUR OWN machine (a missing tool permission, folder access, or sign-in) is yours to resolve with your own operator \u2014 report it as your side being blocked; never ask another member to change your machine.";
